excerpt: "On a warm Saturday morning in the second week of August, at nine-oh-six, at a small Colonial-Red plywood stand at the crossroads of Franklin Pike and Duncan Gap Road in Floyd County, Virginia, an eighty-two-year-old widow named Marion 'Marnie' Whitcomb Halloran sets out twelve quart mason jars of peach jam, nineteen brown paper bags of Silver Queen corn, and — as she has since the third weekend of the June of 1987 — turns the small hinged blackboard on the back of the stand around to the crossroads and leaves the honor box, a rusted olive-green cash box her husband Douglas welded shut in the March of 1987 with a single narrow slit at the top, unattended for the day. Somewhere on the second floor of a converted feed store on Franklin Street in Christiansburg, thirty-eight miles down the mountain, on the same morning at nine-thirty-two, the record for M. W. Halloran in the file of the Blue Ridge Community Clinic reads *sustaining $75/month since 6/2001, ACH, mail only, spouse deceased, do not upgrade, tier 4.* A short letter, with a great deal of tenderness, on the small quiet piece of plywood at a crossroads in the Blue Ridge — and on the thirty-nine summers of giving your CRM has been quietly, patiently, without any malice at all, failing to see."
