# The Honor Box at the Farm Stand

Marnie Halloran opens the stand on Memorial Day and closes it after the second frost.

She has done it, at the crossroads of Franklin Pike and Duncan Gap Road in Floyd County, Virginia, on the third weekend of the June of 1987, since the second Saturday after Douglas — her husband Douglas Everett Halloran, cabinetmaker, the Reverend Ephraim Walsh's third son-in-law, who ordered his own set of dovetail chisels from Woodcraft in the September of 1969 and used them until his hand went — was buried in the small stone-fenced cemetery beside Little River Chapel three-quarters of a mile up the pike.

Douglas was sixty-one.

Marnie is now eighty-two.

The stand is a piece of three-quarter-inch birch plywood Douglas cut on the tablesaw in the front room of the small brick ranch house on Franklin Pike in the second week of March of 1987, six weeks after the diagnosis and eleven weeks before the end, and painted, in a color Rustoleum called *Colonial Red* for reasons Douglas found quietly funny in the way he found most things quietly funny, in a single careful afternoon on the second Saturday of April.

He built her the honor box, too — a rusted olive-green metal box he cut from the sheet steel he had left over from the mailbox posts he had built along the pike in the summer of 1983 — and welded it shut in the March of 1987, with only a single narrow slit at the top just wide enough for a folded dollar bill.

He told her, on the porch of the small brick ranch in the second week of April, with a warm cup of Bengal Spice in his good hand and the bad hand in his lap, that he wanted her to have somewhere to put a little something for the clinic in Christiansburg for a while.

He said it once.

She has taken him at his word for thirty-nine summers.

## What Marnie brings on Saturday mornings

Every Saturday morning in the season, at eight-forty-two, in a fifteen-year-old red Ford Ranger with a small dent in the tailgate her grandson Silas put there with a mailbox in the fall of 2018, Marnie drives the mile and three tenths from the small brick ranch on Franklin Pike to the crossroads at Duncan Gap.

She brings, in three plastic milk crates in the truck bed:

Twelve quart mason jars of the peach jam she puts up on the second week of every August from the four trees behind the smokehouse. Nineteen brown paper bags of Silver Queen corn her nephew Wyatt drops off on Fridays from the bottom fields of his father's place near Copper Hill. Six pints of the strawberry preserves she puts up on the third Saturday of every June with her sister Bess from the plants at the far edge of the front yard. Twelve small plastic clamshells of the tomatoes her neighbor Freda Wickham brings her from the raised beds behind Freda's blue Craftsman on Rocky Knob Road. Nine folded terracotta plant markers on which Marnie has, in the small careful hand her fourth-grade teacher Mrs. Winifred Kegley taught her in the winter of 1953, written the varieties: *Cherokee Purple. Green Zebra. Better Boy. Aunt Ruby's German Green.*

She sets them out on the plywood in the same order she has set them out since a Saturday in the June of 1987.

She writes, in blue chalk on a small hinged blackboard Douglas built into the back of the stand, the price of the day:

*Jam $6. Corn $4/half dozen. Preserves $7. Tomatoes $4. Basil $2.*

And in smaller letters at the bottom, in a hand that has grown a little slower since the winter of 2023:

*All proceeds to the Blue Ridge Community Clinic in Christiansburg. Thank you kindly for stopping.*

Then she gets back in the red Ranger and drives home, and the stand is on the honor system from nine-oh-six on Saturday morning until the last light of Saturday afternoon — which, in Floyd County in the second week of August, is somewhere near eight forty-seven.

## The little green box

At the far right end of the plywood, bolted through with three quarter-inch carriage bolts to the two-by-four frame Douglas milled at his own bench in the shop, is the little green box.

At the top of the box, a single narrow slit just wide enough for a folded dollar bill.

At the bottom of the box, a small handwritten label — the same label Douglas made her write out with her own hand on a Wednesday afternoon in the second week of March of 1987, on cream stock she cut from a piece of the mat board he used for his framed clock faces:

*All proceeds to the Blue Ridge Community Clinic — 148 Franklin Street, Christiansburg. Thank you kindly.*

The box was not built to be opened.

Since a warm Sunday afternoon in the last week of September of 1987, Marnie has, on the last Sunday of every month in the season, unbolted the box from the plywood at the crossroads with the small ratchet Douglas kept on the second shelf of the shop, taken it up to the ranch on Franklin Pike, driven the mile and a half down Rocky Knob Road to the Chevron station where her cousin Ralph Whitcomb has run the register since the winter of 1979, and had Ralph — who has never once asked her why — cut the top off with the bench grinder in the back of the shop.

Ralph has done it, on the last Sunday of every month in every season, four hundred and thirty-one times.

Inside the box, folded into small quiet squares by the hands of thousands of strangers over thirty-nine summers, are — depending on the week — anywhere from forty-two to two hundred and eleven dollars.

She unfolds them on Sunday night at the small oak kitchen table under the north window of the ranch with a fresh cup of Constant Comment. She writes the weekly total on the next line of a Wilson Jones green ledger book Ralph gave her in the November of 1988 and has been sending her a fresh copy of every seven years since. She writes, on Monday morning, a single check drawn on the joint account at Skyline National Bank on Locust Street in Floyd, made out to *Blue Ridge Community Clinic,* and mails it in a plain white envelope from the post office at the corner of West Main and Locust in the little town of Floyd.

She has mailed one hundred and seventy-eight of them since the June of 1987.

The check for the first week of August of 2026 was for eight hundred and sixty-two dollars.

The average, across nineteen weeks of every summer for thirty-nine summers, is one thousand one hundred and forty-one.

Nine thousand a summer.

*Three hundred and forty-two thousand dollars,* in checks drawn on a joint account still bearing Douglas Halloran's name, in the file of the Blue Ridge Community Clinic in Christiansburg — mailed from a plywood stand at a crossroads by the widow of a cabinetmaker with a bad hand and a rusted olive-green box he welded shut in March of 1987 because he wanted her to have somewhere to put the money for a while.

## What the folder in Christiansburg says

On the second floor of a converted feed store at 148 Franklin Street in Christiansburg, thirty-eight miles down the mountain and one long two-lane switchback off the parkway from Marnie's ranch, is a folder.

The label at the top of the folder, in a script that has been faded since 2007, reads *Halloran, M.W.*

Inside the folder is:

*Sustaining donor, $75/month by ACH since 6/2001. Mail-only correspondence. Spouse deceased. Do not upgrade. Tier 4.*

That is the whole record.

There is no field on the record for the check that arrives on the eleventh of every summer month from a P.O. box near Floyd.

There is no field on the record for the fact that between the June of 1987 and the June of 2001 — the year the clinic first began accepting ACH — the same joint account mailed the same clinic one hundred and seventy-six plain white envelopes, none of which were ever entered.

There is no field on the record for the fact that in the March of 1987 Douglas Everett Halloran, sixty-one, cabinetmaker, was carried up the stairs of the same feed store by his two brothers-in-law and treated on the second floor for the eleven weeks after his diagnosis by a country doctor named Nathan Sanborn, seventy-two, who wrote in the discharge note on the second-to-last day of Douglas's life: *asked only about his wife.*

There is no field on the record for the fact that in the November of 2024, at eighty, Marnie signed on a warm Thursday afternoon at the kitchen table with an attorney named Mary Beth Pruett a plain three-page will leaving the small brick ranch house on Franklin Pike, the thirteen acres behind it, the smokehouse, the peach trees, the shop with Douglas's dovetail chisels still hanging on the pegboard above the workbench, and the residuary of a joint account with three hundred and forty-two thousand dollars in it — to the Blue Ridge Community Clinic in Christiansburg.

There is no field, in the file marked *Halloran, M.W.,* for a piece of Colonial-Red plywood at a crossroads.

## The person on your ACH list is not always the whole donor

Here is a small thing the sector has been quietly getting wrong for twenty years.

The person on your sustaining-donor list is not always the whole donor.

Sometimes — often, if you are a small rural clinic in the mountains, or a food pantry off a two-lane road, or a hospice that once treated a cabinetmaker with a bad hand — the person on your sustaining-donor list is *one small window* onto a life that has three of them, and has, for reasons your paper form has never once asked about, painted the other two Colonial Red.

The whole record you have on Marnie Halloran is the $75 a month you asked for on a photocopied form in the June of 2001.

The whole gift, across thirty-nine summers, is somewhere north of fifty times that.

The whole gift, when it arrives after the second frost of a fall you cannot yet name, is somewhere north of five hundred times that.

And the whole story — the shop, the chisels, the March of 1987, the bad hand, the porch, the Bengal Spice — was on the front porch of a small brick ranch on Franklin Pike, on a warm afternoon a mile and a half from your office, in the second week of April of 1987, in a single quiet sentence a dying cabinetmaker said once and never had cause to say again.

The industry has been teaching you to segment him out for twenty years.

## What Rōmy sees at nine-thirty-two

Rōmy reads the small papers.

It reads, on a warm Saturday morning in the second week of August, at nine-oh-eight, Douglas Everett Halloran's obituary on page six of the *Floyd Press* of the fourth week of the June of 1987 — the one that names the Blue Ridge Community Clinic in Christiansburg in its final line and asks that remembrances be sent care of Reverend Ephraim Walsh at Little River Chapel. It reads a small line on the second page of the *Floyd County Extension Newsletter* of the August of 1987 that thanks Marion Halloran of Franklin Pike for beginning a summer honor-system produce stand at the Duncan Gap crossroads in memory of her husband. It reads a paragraph in the fall 2011 *Blue Ridge Community Clinic Annual Report* honoring, among its longstanding physicians, the late Dr. Nathan Sanborn, who died in the March of 2011 in the same second-floor room he treated Douglas Halloran in twenty-four Marches earlier. And it reads, on a small line under *warranty deed transfers* in the *Floyd Press* of the second week of the December of 2024, the recorded transfer of a life estate on three acres of Franklin Pike land from Marion W. Halloran to the Blue Ridge Community Clinic — the small careful piece of paper that means the will has, in effect, already begun.

Then, at nine-thirty-two on that same Saturday morning, it puts on the desk of a twenty-eight-year-old development coordinator at the clinic — a woman named Grace Alderman Peebles — a single sourced page.

*M. W. Halloran (Marnie), our $75/month ACH sustaining donor since 6/2001, is the widow of Douglas Everett Halloran (d. 6/1987), whom our founder Dr. Nathan Sanborn cared for on our second floor in the spring of 1987. She has, since the June of 1987, run an honor-system produce stand at the crossroads of Franklin Pike and Duncan Gap Road with all proceeds to this clinic. We have received approximately $342,000 from that stand across 39 summers by unentered paper checks. She recorded a life-estate transfer to the clinic on 3 acres of Franklin Pike land in December of 2024. Suggested: one plain cream card, this week, in your own hand — not from Development, from you — to Marnie at 1148 Franklin Pike, mentioning Douglas by name once, mentioning Dr. Sanborn once, mentioning the Colonial-Red plywood once. No ask. No case for support. Ask, at the very end, whether she would let you drive up the pike some Saturday morning in September to help her carry the jars out to the stand. Would you like a draft, in your own voice?*

Grace writes the card by hand on a Monday afternoon and mails it Tuesday from the post office at Depot Street.

Marnie reads it on Wednesday at the kitchen table in a green cardigan Douglas bought her at Belk's in Roanoke in the summer of 1979, with a fresh cup of Bengal Spice and the cash-box lid — cut off the day before — in the small maple tray beside the ledger.

She sits with it for a long quiet minute.

Then she calls her daughter Rebecca in Roanoke and reads it aloud.

## We are not the plywood

Rōmy is not the plywood at the crossroads of Franklin Pike and Duncan Gap Road.

Rōmy is not the olive-green cash box welded shut on a Wednesday afternoon in the March of 1987, or the terracotta plant markers, or the Aunt Ruby's German Green tomatoes, or the Wilson Jones green ledger book with one hundred and seventy-eight small careful entries in it, or the porch on Franklin Pike in the second week of April with a cup of Bengal Spice in a good hand and a bad hand in a lap.

Rōmy is the small careful page a good listener would have put on Grace Alderman Peebles's desk on a warm Saturday morning in the August of 2026, if a good listener had been sitting on the porch of the small brick ranch in the second week of April of 1987, and in the second-floor room over the feed store on Franklin Street in the spring of that year, and beside the plywood at the Duncan Gap crossroads every Saturday morning at nine-oh-six for thirty-nine summers.

The whole product is a smaller, warmer job than the industry has been trying to sell for twenty years.

It is not to prospect Marnie. It is to *notice* her.

## A small assignment for the officer reading this on a Sunday

Before the peach trees behind the smokehouse are picked for the last time this summer, do one small unglamorous thing this week.

Pull, out of your CRM, your ten longest-tenured $75-a-month sustaining donors. Not the tier ones. Not the wealth-screened ones. The *paper-form-in-a-drawer* ones. The ones you have been debiting $75 from since the summer of 2001 and have never once asked what small quiet thing they built at a crossroads in the summer of the year they lost somebody.

Read the memorial line of the local paper obituary for the year the ACH started, and for the summer before.

You will find, on more Saturday mornings than the sector has been telling you, an eighty-two-year-old widow at a crossroads in Floyd County, Virginia, with a Wilson Jones green ledger book and a rusted olive-green cash box welded shut in the March of 1987, in the middle of a quiet stewardship that is going to move a small brick ranch, thirteen acres, a smokehouse, a set of dovetail chisels, and three hundred and forty-two thousand dollars in your direction — because a cabinetmaker with a bad hand once said one sentence on a porch on Franklin Pike in the second week of April.

Then, from your own kitchen table, on a plain cream envelope with a real stamp — write her one page in your own hand.

Do not ask her for anything.

Say her husband's name once. Say the Colonial-Red plywood once. Sign it *warmly.*

Ask, at the very end, whether she would let you drive up the pike some Saturday morning in September to help her carry the jars out to the stand.

Mail it Tuesday.

The stand is going to be at the crossroads of Franklin Pike and Duncan Gap on Saturday morning at nine-oh-six, the way it has been on Saturday mornings at nine-oh-six since a warm Saturday in the June of 1987.

Somebody's name is going to go on the envelope she folds and pushes through the narrow slit at the top of the little green box.

Turn to her. ♡
